const List<TechniqueSection> techAccentBasicsLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why",
    body:
        "An accent tells the listener where the beat is. Before accents can move around, they have to sit on the counts, loud and clear, while everything in between stays soft and even. This is where a stream of notes turns into music.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How",
    body:
        "Accented note: a full stroke from high (about 25 cm). Unaccented notes: low taps (about 5 cm). Count out loud, \"1 and 2 and\", and let the accent land exactly on the number. The hand that plays the accent lifts early enough to be high in time; the stroke itself is still a throw, not a hit.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Tips",
    body:
        "The notes right after an accent tend to get loud too: keep them down. Accents do not rush, they land in time. Lines 4 and 7 put the accents in the left hand, give it the same height. Line 8 moves the accent to 2 and 4: the backbeat, the heart of almost every rock and pop groove.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Song examples",
    body:
        "Superstition (Stevie Wonder): the hi-hat is a stream of sixteenths with the weight on the beat. Walk This Way (Aerosmith): the hi-hat intro, accented eighths. Marching band snare cadences: every count accented, everything else quiet.",
  ),
];
